TY  - JOUR
TI  - BERTRAG as a BERT-enhanced retrieval-augmented generation framework for preserving Awori and Ogu indigenous knowledge
AU  - Olusola Olabanjo
AU  - Ashiribo Wusu
AU  - Oseni Afisi
T2  - Scientific Reports
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-09-16
DO  - 10.1038/s41598-026-69728-4
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-69728-4
AB  - Indigenous cultural knowledge is frequently distributed across heterogeneous and low-resource sources, including oral histories, archival records, ritual texts, community narratives, and locally produced documents. Such knowledge is often context-dependent and governed by community protocols, making it susceptible to distortion, hallucination, and loss of contextual meaning when processed using generic large language models (LLMs). This study presents BERTRAG, a BERT-enhanced Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework for supporting the preservation and responsible use of indigenous knowledge, evaluated through a case study of the Awori and Ogu communities of Lagos, Nigeria. BERTRAG integrates a community-curated, governance-aware corpus, domain-adapted BERT embeddings for culturally informed semantic retrieval, and retrieval-conditioned generation with explicit provenance and abstention constraints to promote evidence-grounded responses and reduce hallucination. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that domain-adapted retrieval improves Recall@10 from 0.61 for BM25, 0.68 for generic BERT, and 0.72 for the contemporary E5-base-v2 dense embedding baseline to 0.77 for BERTRAG. Retrieval-grounded generation achieves a faithfulness score of 91.3% and reduces the hallucination rate from 35.8% for the LLM-only baseline to 8.7% for BERTRAG. Expert evaluation further demonstrates improved cultural fidelity, with mean overall fidelity scores of 3.0 for the LLM-only baseline and 3.9 for the Retrieval + Extractive QA baseline, compared with 4.6 for BERTRAG on a five-point Likert scale. The framework also exhibits calibrated abstention behavior under low-evidence conditions, supporting cautious responses when reliable supporting evidence is unavailable. Within the scope of the Awori and Ogu case study, these findings indicate that combining domain-adapted retrieval, provenance-aware generation, explicit abstention, and culturally informed evaluation can improve the reliability and cultural appropriateness of RAG for indigenous knowledge question answering. The proposed framework offers a candidate methodological approach for culturally governed, low-resource knowledge domains, although its applicability beyond the communities studied remains to be established through future empirical evaluation.
